    {
      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "2035369569049608323",
      "url": "https://x.com/MCreekFutures/status/2035369569049608323",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/MCreekFutures/status/2035369569049608323",
      "text": "Lean Hog (/HE) Market Outlook & News\n\nCurrent Date: March 20, 2026\n\nThe market enters the third weekend of March at a critical technical inflection point. While the early-week momentum looked promising, a \"Friday Flush\" saw lean hog futures slip lower, pressured by a broader sell-off across the livestock complex and a massive spike in energy prices. Despite the futures retreat, the fundamental cash story remains resilient: the CME Lean Hog Index continues its steady ascent, and the pork carcass cutout has staged a remarkable recovery, nearly reclaiming the $100 mark after a mid-week dip.\n\nMarket Intelligence: The New Trade Map\nThe \"Efficiency Paradox\" is being tested by geopolitical volatility. As energy prices surge, the cost of production and transport is once again a primary concern for the global trade map:\n\nEnergy & Grain Spillover: Brent Crude jumped above $119 a barrel following Middle East energy facility attacks. This has tethered corn prices to the energy rally, increasing feed costs and squeezing the \"crush\" for producers.\n\nChina’s Domestic Record: China reported record pork production of 59.38 million tons for 2025. \n\nConsequently, their imports have plummeted nearly 78% compared to 2020 levels. They are currently targeting a further reduction of 1.4 million sows to rebalance their oversupplied domestic market.\n\nThe Brazil Surge: Brazil has solidified its position as the world’s third-largest exporter. They are aggressively capturing market share in the Philippines (projected up 7% in 2026) and Mexico, leveraging their low-cost production model and PRRS-free status.\n\n1. Supply & Demand: The Tug-of-War\n\nSlaughter Contraction: USDA estimated Thursday’s federally inspected hog slaughter at 492,000 head, with the week-to-date total at 1.879 million. This is a significant 89,000 head below last week’s pace, though it remains about 78,000 head above last year. This tightening week-over-week supply is providing the necessary floor for cash prices.\n\nCutout Volatility: The Friday AM report saw the carcass cutout value jump $2.84 back to $100.89. This recovery was broad-based, with the belly primal leading the charge with a $6.96 gain, followed by strong support in butts and loins.\n2. The \"Disease Floor\" & Logistics\n\nHealth Alert: The March Swine Disease Reporting System (SDRS) confirms that while overall PRRSV positivity saw a moderate decrease to 43.7%, it remains at high-stress levels. PEDV positivity, however, has moved three standard deviations above the baseline in Illinois, signaling a localized but severe threat to spring piglet supplies.\n\nCash-Futures Convergence: The national base hog price was reported at $91.29 on Friday morning. With the CME Lean Hog Index at $92.04, the spread between cash and the April futures contract ($91.42) has effectively collapsed, indicating the market is fully caught up to the current reality.\n3. Record Export Velocity\n\nWeekly Sales Bounce: The March 19 Export Sales report showed new pork business of 28,284 MT, up nearly 20% from the previous week. Mexico remains the undisputed anchor, taking 7,500 MT, while South Korea showed renewed appetite with 5,700 MT.\n\nShipment Lag: Physical shipments hit a 4-week low of 35,674 MT, likely due to higher domestic prices and logistics adjustments following the energy spike. However, the year-to-date pace remains well ahead of the 5-year average.\nTechnical Forecast: Monday Open (March 23)\n\nBullish Case: April Hogs (HEJ26) closed Friday at $91.42, down over $2.00 on the week. Bulls must defend the $91.00 level to prevent a deeper slide. A recovery back above $93.50 is needed to repair the technical damage from the late-week liquidation.\n\nBearish Case: If the broad \"livestock sell-off\" continues, the next major support for April sits at $89.50. Watch for continued \"long-liquidation\" if open interest continues to drop.\n\nCommitment of Traders: Managed money cut 11,151 contracts from their net long position this week. While they remain net long 116,553 contracts, the reduction suggests institutional traders are de-risking in the face of global uncertainty.\n\nCurrent Crush Dynamics (March 20, 2026)\nProfit margins faced a \"double-black-swan\" on Friday: falling hog futures combined with rising corn prices due to the crude oil rally.\n\nContract / IndexPrice Change\n\nApril Lean Hogs (HEJ26)$91.42-0.62\nMay Lean Hogs (HEK26)$95.70-0.35\nJune Lean Hogs (HEM26)$104.42-0.32\n\nCME Lean Hog Index$92.04+0.11\n\nPork Carcass Cutout$100.89+2.84\n\nMay Corn (ZCK26)$4.68+0.08May Soy Meal (ZMK26)$314.50-3.90\n\nAnalysis Note: We are currently in a \"Negative Basis\" environment for the April contract, with the Index ($92.04) trading higher than the futures ($91.42). Historically, this is a highly bullish signal, as the futures market is effectively \"too cheap\" relative to the actual cash being paid for hogs. \n\nUnless the cash market breaks over the weekend, look for a technical \"snap-back\" rally early next week as futures are forced to chase the rising index.\n#hogs #leanhogs #pork",
